{{/*
family: rsyslog
what: syslog
transports: udp, tcp, tls
reference: rsyslog documentation: the omfwd action (target, port, protocol, TCP_Framing, StreamDriver*), RFC 5424 and RFC 5425
verification: tested
ci: CI saves this file for real rsyslog, over UDP, TCP and TLS (Woodshed's own CA), over IPv4 and over IPv6 (TCP), with a chosen severity and facility (UDP), and with Woodshed down until the queue overflows (TCP and TLS). The test messages go to rsyslog's own UDP input rather than through the logger line.
*/}}
{{define "does"}}Forwards this machine's syslog{{if .Facility}} ({{.Facility}} only){{end}}, {{.Severity.Word}} and more severe, to Woodshed over {{if eq .Transport "udp"}}UDP{{else if eq .Transport "tcp"}}TCP{{else}}TLS{{end}}.{{end}}

{{define "parts"}}
@@part file /etc/rsyslog.d/60-woodshed.conf Save as this file, then restart rsyslog (sudo systemctl restart rsyslog)
# Woodshed: forward syslog over {{if eq .Transport "udp"}}UDP{{else if eq .Transport "tcp"}}TCP{{else}}TLS{{end}} (RFC 5424 format).
{{if .Facility}}{{.Facility}}{{else}}*{{end}}.{{.Severity.Name}} action(type="omfwd" target="{{.Address}}" port="{{.Port}}"
{{- if eq .Transport "udp"}} protocol="udp"
{{- else}} protocol="tcp" TCP_Framing="octet-counted"{{end}}
{{- if eq .Transport "tls"}}
       StreamDriver="gtls" StreamDriverMode="1" StreamDriver.CAFile="<CA-FILE>"
       StreamDriverAuthMode="x509/name" StreamDriverPermittedPeers="{{.TLSName}}"{{end}}
       template="RSYSLOG_SyslogProtocol23Format"
{{- if ne .Transport "udp"}}
       queue.type="LinkedList" queue.size="10000" queue.timeoutEnqueue="0" action.resumeRetryCount="-1"{{end}})
@@part command - Check the file and send a test message
sudo rsyslogd -N1
logger -p user.{{if eq .Severity.Name "debug"}}debug{{else}}{{.Severity.Name}}{{end}} "Test message for Woodshed from $(hostname)"
{{end}}

{{define "notes"}}
{{if eq .Severity.Name "emerg"}}The test message is an emergency: rsyslog's default rules write those to every logged-in user's terminal.
{{end}}{{if .Facility}}The test message uses facility user, which the {{.Facility}} filter doesn't forward: use `logger -p {{.Facility}}.{{.Severity.Name}}` to test.
{{end}}
{{- if eq .Transport "tls"}}
<CA-FILE> is Woodshed's CA certificate as a PEM file on this machine{{if .TLSOwnCA}}: download it from Settings → TLS → CA certificate (PEM), for example to /etc/rsyslog.d/woodshed-ca.pem (some distributions confine rsyslogd to reading its own configuration folders, so keep it there){{if .CASHA256}}, and check its SHA-256 fingerprint is {{.CASHA256}}{{end}}{{else}}: this Woodshed uses your own certificate, so use the CA that issued it{{end}}.
{{if .TLSNameKnown}}rsyslog checks that Woodshed's certificate holds the name {{.TLSName}}{{if not .IsName}} (it doesn't check IP addresses){{end}}.{{else if eq .TLSName "<WOODSHED-TLS-NAME>"}}<WOODSHED-TLS-NAME> is a DNS name on Woodshed's TLS certificate (Settings → TLS); rsyslog checks names, not IP addresses. Turn TLS on first to see them.{{else}}rsyslog checks that Woodshed's certificate holds the name {{.TLSName}}. This Woodshed has no certificate names to compare yet: once TLS is on, check that Settings → TLS lists {{.TLSName}}.{{end}}
TLS needs rsyslog 8.2108 or later and its gtls network stream driver, which some distributions package separately.
{{- end}}
{{if ne .Transport "udp"}}While Woodshed can't be reached, up to 10,000 messages wait in the in-memory queue and rsyslog retries until it can. Once the queue is full, new messages for Woodshed are dropped (queue.timeoutEnqueue="0") rather than holding up the rest of the system's logging; local log files carry on, and a restart of rsyslog loses what's waiting.
{{end}}
{{- if eq .Transport "udp"}}UDP has no delivery check: a message lost on the way is gone. Use TCP or TLS where that matters.
{{end}}
{{end}}

{{define "check"}}On Woodshed's Dashboard this machine appears as a device within a few seconds of the test message; `woodshed status` counts the messages too.{{end}}
